{"title":"Fiber product","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>fiber product</b>, also called the <b>pullback</b>, is an idea in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Category_theory\" title=\"Category theory\">category theory</a> which occurs in many areas of mathematics.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition\">Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fiber_product&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> be objects of the same category; let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/1/b81302319cdd786252eb9908c5ccf26c601343c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi : X \\to Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/b/09bf9ce7f371d88edeec4eb87ed39a5f8bb7b75b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi : Y \\to Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" /> be <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Homomorphism\" title=\"Homomorphism\">homomorphisms</a> of this category.  Then the fiber product of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/a/3ead47fb9fb4a4c273feee398f72ff2a09702b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Z$\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" />, denoted <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c7fec378ac8a39c63b486cc991559acad38566.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\times_Z Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" /> (when the specific functions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ada738001410f131563551fb68731e4f302048d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"12\" height=\"16\" /> are clear) is the set of elements <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> in the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Limit_(category_theory)&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Limit (category theory) (page does not exist)\">product</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a987d6c74c4ee50b1aa34793646f97bbf2cd30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X \\times Y$\" width=\"52\" height=\"12\" /> in which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/5/5d537cfe343b7f94e4b7b50b71dca8bb93349cf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(x) = \\psi(y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Fiber_product&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261